WEBVTT

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.890
Sofia: Could we shift everyone to four days without dropping the ball on

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.510
coverage, customers or deadlines, and what would you want to test first?

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.344
Priya: That's the big question, Sofia.

00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:26.771
My main concern is the cost of any productivity dip.

00:00:26.821 --> 00:00:30.540
If we're paying full salaries for twenty percent less time, I

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.310
need to see how that hits our margins before we commit.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.055
Daniel: Priya's right to worry about the cost, but from

00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:39.388
my side, the bigger risk is the contracts.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:42.305
If we change hours, we need to make sure we're

00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:44.930
not breaching any service level agreements with our clients.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.380
Sofia: That's a fair point, Daniel.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.838
If we can't guarantee the same response times, the whole thing falls apart.

00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:52.764
I'd suggest we run a small pilot with one team first

00:00:52.814 --> 00:00:55.690
to see if we can maintain coverage without burning everyone out.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.010
Marcus: I'm with Sofia...

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.210
You: Uh, Priya, what would a pilot actually cost us?

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:06.377
Priya: If we keep salaries the same, the direct cost is the loss of those hours.

00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:10.693
I'd need to look at the specific team's payroll to give you a hard number,

00:01:10.743 --> 00:01:15.010
but the real cost is whether we have to hire contractors to fill the gaps.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.423
Leo: I see the cost side, but think about the story here.

00:01:19.473 --> 00:01:24.107
Being the company that actually pulls this off would be a massive

00:01:24.157 --> 00:01:28.400
magnet for top talent and a huge win for our brand.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.533
Marcus: Leo's right about the talent pull, but we can't let

00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:34.244
the brand win blind us to the actual experience.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.257
If we just cram five days of work into four,

00:01:37.307 --> 00:01:40.270
we'll just be trading one kind of burnout for another.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.590
Leo: That's a fair...

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:40.628
You: Legal.

00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:44.010
Is there anything here that could get us in trouble?

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.300
Daniel: The main thing is those service level agreements.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.640
If we've promised clients twenty four seven support or specific turnaround

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.677
times, a four day week could put us in breach.

00:01:55.727 --> 00:01:58.410
I'd need to review our current contracts to see

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.840
where we're exposed before we greenlight a pilot.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:01.920
Sofia: That's the hurdle.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.520
If we can't figure out...

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:07.620
You: Can someone check what the Big Four day week trials actually found though?

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.028
Sofia: I can look that up for you right now.

00:02:14.078 --> 00:02:16.376
those big trials generally showed that productivity stayed the

00:02:16.426 --> 00:02:18.725
same or even went up, and burnout dropped significantly.

00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:21.073
Most companies that tried it actually ended up keeping

00:02:21.123 --> 00:02:23.160
it because the retention was so much better.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:27.509
Priya: That sounds great on paper, but those trials often measure productivity differently.

00:02:27.559 --> 00:02:32.091
I'd want to know if that means the same output was achieved, or

00:02:32.141 --> 00:02:36.320
if they just stopped doing certain tasks to make the numbers work.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:37.720
Sofia: That's a fair challenge...

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.479
You: Let's run the pilot, Leo.

00:02:39.529 --> 00:02:42.250
How do we tell the team?

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:49.572
Leo: We frame this as an investment in them, not a test of their speed.

00:02:49.622 --> 00:02:53.891
We tell them we're piloting a way to work smarter, not harder, and

00:02:53.941 --> 00:02:58.210
that the goal is to protect their time while keeping our clients happy.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.999
Marcus: I like that framing, Leo.

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:03.002
But if we're calling it an investment, we've got

00:03:03.052 --> 00:03:05.672
to be crystal clear about the success metrics.

00:03:05.722 --> 00:03:09.343
People will be anxious that the rug's being pulled out if

00:03:09.393 --> 00:03:12.680
they don't know exactly how we're measuring that smarter work.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:13.367
Sofia: I agree.

00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:17.335
If we don't define what success looks like before we start, we're just guessing.

00:03:17.385 --> 00:03:21.870
We need a hard set of output metrics so the team knows exactly where they stand.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.590
Daniel: That's where the risk lives.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.790
If the metrics are vague, we're open to claims of

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.670
unfair treatment if we decide to roll it back.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:29.990
We...
